me chord of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the inscribed angles subtended by \\(AB\\) in the two circles are equal:\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BFA=\\angle BAE.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle(AP,AB)\\). Combining (6) and (7) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle\\bigl(\\, \\text{line }H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\; HB\\,\\bigr)=\\angle BFA .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (8) is precisely the condition for the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to be tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\): the angle made by a tangent with a chord equals the angle subtended by the same chord at the opposite point of the circle. Hence the required tangency holds. ∎  \n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the two intersecting circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nDenote by \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points, by \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles (the order on the line is \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Spiral similarity at \\(A\\))\n\nBecause the circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we have \\(\\mathcal S(X)\\in\\Gamma\\) and \\(A,X,\\mathcal S(X)\\) are collinear.  \nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal S(E)=F,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince both \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the set \\(MN\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\); likewise the chord \\(AB\\) is invariant because its endpoints are fixed. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\))\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the side \\(CD\\).  \nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of \\(CD\\); then  \n\n\\[\nPO\\perp CD\\;(=MN).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Location of the orthocentre \\(H\\))\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from the vertex \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\).  \nBy (2.1) this altitude coincides with the line \\(PO\\); therefore the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on \\(PO\\).  \n\nMoreover, the altitude from \\(M\\) meets the line \\(PN\\) at the reflection of \\(O\\) in \\(PN\\); reflecting \\(O\\) in \\(PN\\) yields precisely the orthocentre \\(H\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nH\\ \\text{is the reflection of }O\\text{ in the line }PN.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (The midpoint \\(O\\) lies on the common chord \\(AB\\))\n\nApply the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) of Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(APO\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes \\(A\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), it sends the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) to the midpoint of the segment joining \\(\\mathcal S(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal S(D)\\).  \nBut \\(\\mathcal S(D)\\) is the point of \\(\\Omega\\) that corresponds to \\(D\\); denote it by \\(C'\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the chord \\(AB\\), the points \\(C\\) and \\(C'\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AB\\). Consequently the segment \\(DC\\) is mapped onto the segment \\(C'D\\), which is the reflection of \\(DC\\) across \\(AB\\). The midpoint of a segment and the midpoint of its reflection across a line coincide on that line; therefore the image of \\(O\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is again \\(O\\).  \n\nA point that is fixed by a non‑trivial spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) must lie on the invariant line of the similarity. The only line invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\) that does **not** pass through \\(A\\) is the common chord \\(AB\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nO\\in AB .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Collinearity \\(A,B,H\\))\n\nFrom Lemma 3 we have \\(H\\in PO\\).  \nLemma 2 gives \\(PO\\perp MN\\); Lemma 4 tells us that \\(O\\in AB\\).  \nThus the line \\(PO\\) passes through the point \\(O\\) of the chord \\(AB\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the line \\(MN\\) (Lemma 1) and also fixes the point \\(A\\); therefore it fixes the unique line through \\(A\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). That line is precisely \\(AP\\). Since \\(P\\) lies on it, the whole line \\(AP\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \n\nBecause \\(O\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\) (Lemma 4) and lies on the invariant line \\(AB\\), the only line through \\(O\\) that can be invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the line \\(AB\\) itself. Consequently the line \\(PO\\) coincides with \\(AB\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nH\\in PO=AB\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad A,B,H\\text{ are collinear}.\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (Angle equality)\n\nSince \\(A,B,H\\) are collinear, the directed angle between the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) and the chord \\(HB\\) equals the directed angle between \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\,H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\;HB\\,\\bigr)=\\angle(AP,AB).\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 1 the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the same chord of \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the inscribed angles subtended by \\(AB\\) in the two circles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFA=\\angle BAE .\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BAE=\\angle(AP,AB)\\). Combining (6.1) and (6.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(\\,H\\!-\\!{\\parallel}AP,\\;HB\\,\\bigr)=\\angle BFA .\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion (Tangency)\n\nFor any circle, the angle formed by a tangent at a point with a chord through the point of tangency equals the angle subtended by the same chord at any other point of the circle. Equality (6.3) states exactly that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with the chord \\(HB\\) the same angle as the chord \\(BF\\) subtends at the point \\(F\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Hence that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎"
