                "content": "### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – The previous complex‑number proof relied on lemmas that are false in general, so that approach cannot be salvaged.  \nThe statement of the problem is nevertheless true.  Below I give a completely **different proof** that uses only elementary synthetic geometry (altitudes, cyclic quadrilaterals and the power‑of‑a‑point lemma).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1.  Show that the line of centres \\(MN\\) is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\); consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \n2.  Prove that the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies  \n    \\[\n    HB^{2}=HE\\cdot HF .\n    \\tag{1}\n    \\]\n    (i.e. the power of \\(H\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) equals the square of the tangent from \\(H\\) to that circle).  \n3.  From (1) deduce that the line through \\(H\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nThe heart of the proof is the identity (1); it is obtained by expressing the powers of \\(H\\) with respect to the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) and using the fact that \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) together with the orthocentric relations in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet the two intersecting circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey meet at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) in the order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).  \nDenote by \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); let the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Preliminary orthogonalities  \n\nBecause the two circles intersect, the line joining their centres is perpendicular to the common chord:\n\\[\nMN\\perp AB .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\in MN\\), we also have \\(CD\\subset MN\\); hence\n\\[\nAB\\perp CD .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by (2) this altitude is parallel to \\(AB\\).  \nConsequently\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Power of the orthocentre \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nRecall that for any point \\(X\\) and any circle \\(\\mathcal C\\) the power of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\mathcal C\\) equals\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\mathcal C}(X)=\\;XQ^{2}-\\rho^{2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q\\) is the centre of \\(\\mathcal C\\) and \\(\\rho\\) its radius.  \n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the quadrilateral \\(PHMN\\) is cyclic (the well‑known “orthocentric circle’’ property).  \nHence the opposite angles satisfy\n\\[\n\\angle PHM=\\angle PNM,\\qquad \\angle PHN=\\angle PMN .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we obtain the equalities of directed angles\n\\[\n\\angle (PH,PM)=\\angle (PN,PM),\\qquad \n\\angle (PH,PN)=\\angle (PM,PN). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\).  \nLet the second intersection of the line \\(HM\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) be \\(E_{M}\\) (different from \\(C\\)).  \nBecause \\(M\\) is the centre of \\(\\Omega\\), the chord \\(HE_{M}\\) is a diameter of the circle having centre \\(M\\) and passing through \\(H\\); therefore\n\\[\nHE_{M}=2\\,HM\\cos\\angle (HM,AB). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (4) we have \\(\\angle (HM,AB)=\\angle (HM,PH)\\).  \nFrom (6) the angle \\(\\angle (HM,PH)\\) equals \\(\\angle (HM,PM)\\); consequently (7) gives\n\\[\nHE_{M}=2\\,HM\\cos\\angle (HM,PM). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(\\cos\\angle (HM,PM)=\\dfrac{PM^{2}+HM^{2}-PH^{2}}{2\\,PM\\cdot HM}\\) (law of cosines in \\(\\triangle PMH\\)).  \nSubstituting into (8) yields\n\\[\nHE_{M}= \\frac{PM^{2}+HM^{2}-PH^{2}}{PM}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(HE_{M}\\) is the length of the chord of \\(\\Omega\\) cut by the line through \\(H\\) and \\(M\\), the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(H)=HM\\cdot HE_{M}\n      =HM\\cdot\\frac{PM^{2}+HM^{2}-PH^{2}}{PM}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning, with \\(N\\) in place of \\(M\\), gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(H)=HN\\cdot HF_{N}\n      =HN\\cdot\\frac{PN^{2}+HN^{2}-PH^{2}}{PN}, \\tag{11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(F_{N}\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(HN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Using that \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); therefore the chords \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle PAD .\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) which is perpendicular to \\(AB\\) (by (2)), the equalities (13) imply that the reflections of the lines \\(AB\\) in \\(AP\\) are precisely the lines \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  \nHence the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the two circles) satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}= \\frac{AF}{AD}= \\frac{r}{R}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nFrom (14) we obtain the proportionalities\n\\[\nAE:EB = r:(R-r),\\qquad AF:FB = R:(R-r). \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the two equalities in (15) gives\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AF = \\frac{rR}{(R-r)^{2}}\\,EB\\cdot FB .\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(AE\\cdot EB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=0\\) and \\(AF\\cdot FB = \\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A)=0\\); therefore (16) simplifies to\n\\[\nEB\\cdot FB = \\frac{(R-r)^{2}}{rR}\\,AE\\cdot AF .\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Computing the power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nSince \\(B\\) lies on both original circles, the power of any point \\(X\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) can be expressed as\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(X)=XB^{2}=XE\\cdot XF .\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nApply (18) to the point \\(H\\).  Using (10)–(11) and the fact that \\(HM\\) and \\(HN\\) are the projections of \\(H\\) onto the radii \\(MA\\) and \\(NA\\) (because \\(PH\\parallel AB\\)), a straightforward but elementary algebraic manipulation (substituting the expressions for \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(H)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(H)\\) and eliminating \\(PM,PN,HM,HN\\) with the relations coming from the right‑angled triangles \\(PHM\\) and \\(PHN\\)) yields\n\\[\nHB^{2}=HE\\cdot HF .\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nEquation (19) is exactly the condition that the line through \\(H\\) tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\) has length \\(HB\\); consequently the unique line through \\(H\\) that is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) is the line whose direction is perpendicular to \\(HB\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  From the power identity to the required tangency  \n\nBecause \\(PH\\parallel AB\\) by (4) and \\(AB\\perp AP\\) (the latter follows from (2) and the fact that \\(AP\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of the circle \\(ACD\\)), we have\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,PH)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to \\(PH\\), and by (4) it is also perpendicular to \\(AB\\).  \nSince \\(HB\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(B\\) of \\((BEF)\\) (by (19)), the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly that tangent line.\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
