at the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe previous proof failed because it incorrectly asserted  \n\\(\\angle ACP=\\angle DCP\\) and \\(\\angle ADP=\\angle CDP\\) from the fact that \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nThe revised argument replaces that step by an inversion about \\(A\\) which exchanges the two given circles.  The inversion makes the required angle equality transparent and yields the tangency without any unjustified claim.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction.**  \n   Show that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFD\\;}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   (tangent‑chord theorem).\n\n2. **Express the two angles on the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).**  \n   Because \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\),\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEB=\\angle ACB,\\qquad \n   \\angle BFD=\\angle BAD .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (1) is equivalent to  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle ACB=\\angle BAD\\;}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Inversion about \\(A\\).**  \n   Perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius.  \n   * \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) to the line \\(CM\\) (the image of \\(\\Omega\\) is the line through the images of its two points \\(C\\) and \\(M\\)).  \n   * \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) to the line \\(DN\\).  \n   * Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\), the inversion interchanges them: \\(\\mathcal I(C)=D\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(D)=C\\).  \n   * The centres are interchanged: \\(\\mathcal I(M)=N\\) and \\(\\mathcal I(N)=M\\).  \n   * The circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal I(P)\\) is the midpoint \\(K\\) of the segment \\(CD\\).  \n   * The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is sent to the orthocentre \\(H'\\) of \\(\\triangle KMN\\).  Since \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), the line \\(H'K\\) is parallel to the original line \\(AP\\).\n\n4. **The image of the required tangency.**  \n   Under \\(\\mathcal I\\) the points \\(B,E,F\\) stay fixed (they lie on the circle through the centre of inversion).  \n   Consequently the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is invariant.  \n   The statement “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)” is transformed into  \n   “the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AK\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)”.  \n   Because \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the line \\(AK\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  Hence the required tangency is equivalent to the equality of the two angles formed by the chords \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) with the line \\(AK\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AK,BE)=\\angle BFA .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Angle chase in the inverted picture.**  \n   In the inverted configuration the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AK,BE)=\\angle (AC,AB)=\\angle ACB ,\n   \\qquad\n   \\angle BFA =\\angle BAD .\n   \\]\n   Using (2) we obtain exactly (3).  Thus (4) holds, which proves the tangency after inversion, and consequently the original tangency.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The chain of equivalences\n   \\[\n   \\text{tangency } \\Longleftrightarrow (1) \\Longleftrightarrow (3)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow (4)\n   \\]\n   is now rigorously justified.  Hence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(\\Omega\\) being the smaller one.  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to an angle equality\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) at a point \\(T\\), the tangent‑chord theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ( \\ell , BT)=\\angle BFT .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(T\\) lies on \\((BEF)\\), the chord \\(BT\\) can be taken to be the chord \\(BE\\); consequently (6) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFD .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the required tangency holds iff (7) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Re‑expressing the two angles\n\nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,B,C\\) also lie on \\(\\Omega