o \\((BEF)\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nBecause \\(K\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\), the segment \\(AK\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \nLet \\(\\ell'\\) be the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AK\\).  \nBy the tangent‑chord theorem, \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AK,BE)=\\angle BFA .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIn the inverted configuration the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle (AK,BE)=\\angle (AC,AB)=\\angle ACB ,\\qquad\n\\angle BFA =\\angle BAD .\n\\]\n\nUsing (8)–(9) we obtain exactly the equality (10).  Hence (11) holds, which proves (I).  Because inversion preserves tangency, the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown the chain of equivalences  \n\n\\[\n\\text{tangency of } \\ell \\Longleftrightarrow (7)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (10)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (11)\n\\]\n\nand each step has been justified rigorously.  Therefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
