hat the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe required line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to an angle equality.**  \n   Let \\( \\ell\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Using the tangent‑chord theorem and the facts that \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\), one shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF)\\iff\\boxed{\\;\\angle ACB=\\angle BAD\\;}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Similarity of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle ABD\\).**  \n   Because the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there is a (unique) spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   This similarity carries the point \\(C\\) (the second intersection of the line of centres with \\(\\Omega\\)) to the point \\(D\\) (the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)) and fixes the common point \\(B\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAB=\\angle DAB,\\qquad \n   \\angle CBA=\\angle DBA .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\triangle ABC\\sim\\triangle ABD\\) (AA‑similarity) and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\angle ACB=\\angle BAD\\;}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   From (1) and (2) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) (\\(\\Omega\\) is the smaller one);\n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (\\(PA=PC=PD\\));\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  From tangency to an angle equality\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the angle that \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) equals the angle that \\(AP\\) makes with the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe tangent‑chord theorem states that a line through a point of a circle is tangent to the circle iff the angle it makes with a chord through that point equals the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc.  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) exactly when\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFE . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(A,B,C\\) also lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the inscribed‑angle theorem gives\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle ACB . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(F\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(A,B,D\\) lie on \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle BFE = \\angle BAD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (5) and (6) into (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF)\\iff\\boxed{\\;\\angle ACB=\\angle BAD\\;}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the original statement is equivalent to the angle equality (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Equality \\(\\displaystyle\\angle ACB=\\angle BAD\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique **spiral similarity** with centre \\(A\\) that sends the circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto the circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIndeed, the composition of the homothety with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AN}{AM}\\) followed by a rotation about \\(A\\) maps every point of \\(\\Omega\\) to the corresponding point of \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\nThis spiral similarity has the following properties:\n\n* it sends the centre \\(M\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* it sends the second intersection \\(C\\) of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection \\(D\\) of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* it fixes the common point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) belongs to both circles).\n\nConsequently the oriented angles are preserved:\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAB = \\angle DAB ,\\qquad\n\\angle CBA = \\angle DBA . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the two triangles \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and \\(\\triangle ABD\\) have two equal angles; by the AA‑criterion they are similar, with the correspondence \\(A\\leftrightarrow A,\\;B\\leftrightarrow B,\\;C\\leftrightarrow D\\).  \nTherefore their third angles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle ACB = \\angle BAD\\;}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (7) and (9) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\ell\\text{ (the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{)}\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).\n\\]\n\nHence the required tangency holds for every configuration satisfying the hypotheses.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Atta