arallel to \\(AP\\); but the latter line is precisely the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(H^{\\ast}\\).  \n   Hence the original line through \\(H\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThe three steps give a self‑contained proof that does not use the (incorrect) lemmas of the previous attempt.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An inversion that exchanges the two given circles  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I:\\quad X\\longmapsto X^{\\ast}\n\\]\n\nbe the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AB\\cdot AP .\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) both pass through the centre of the inversion, each of them is transformed into a line.  \nIndeed, for any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{A\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{\\ast}=k=AB\\cdot AP .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AB\\) and \\(AP\\) are the distances from \\(A\\) to the two circles, the set of all points \\(X^{\\ast}\\) with \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) is the line that is the image of \\(\\Omega\\); denote this line by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).  \nAnalogously the image of \\(\\Gamma\\) is a line \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersect at the image of the second common point \\(B\\); because \\(AB\\cdot AB=k\\), we have \\(B^{\\ast}=B\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are precisely the two tangents to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) drawn from \\(B\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}=BE,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}=BF .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) **exchanges the two circles**:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we obtain the following correspondences for the distinguished points:\n\n\\[\nC^{\\ast}=D,\\qquad D^{\\ast}=C,\\qquad\nE^{\\ast}=F,\\qquad F^{\\ast}=E .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n