\n\nSince \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued\nfrom \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles, the line \\(AB\\) is also\nperpendicular to \\(MN\\) (the centres \\(M,N\\) are symmetric with respect to the\nperpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)).  Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line\nthrough \\(P\\) parallel to \\(AB\\).\n\nThe other two altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) intersect this line at a unique\npoint; that point is the orthocentre \\(H\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nH\\in \\ell ,\\qquad\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }P\\text{ parallel to }AB .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circle \\((BEF)\\) and the point \\(P\\)\n\nBecause \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with\n\\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic: the two\nangles subtended by the chord \\(AB\\) at the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are equal\nto the angle subtended by the same chord at the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(A,B,E,F)\\ \\text{are concyclic}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the intersection of the\nperpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  Since the perpendicular bisector\nof \\(BE\\) is the image of the line \\(MN\\) under the homothety with centre\n\\(A\\) and ratio \\(\\dfrac{AB}{AP}\\), and the same holds for \\(BF\\), the two\nbisectors meet at the image of the midpoint \\(O\\) of \\(CD\\) under that same\nhomothety.  By Lemma&nbsp;1, \\(O\\) lies on \\(AB\\); therefore the image of \\(O\\) lies\non the line through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(AB\\), i.e. on the line \\(\\ell\\) defined in\n(2.2).  Consequently the centre of \\((BEF)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell\\).\n\nBecause the centre of \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) and the point \\(P\\) also lies on\n\\(