n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(2,0),\\qquad r_{1}=1,\\qquad r_{2}=2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the two circles intersect because  \n\n\\[\n|r_{2}-r_{1}|=1<d=|MN|=2<r_{1}+r_{2}=3 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nThe equations of the circles are  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=1,\\qquad (x-2)^{2}+y^{2}=4 .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives \\( -4x+4=3\\), i.e. \\(x=\\tfrac14\\).  \nSubstituting into the first circle yields  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=1-\\Bigl(\\tfrac14\\Bigr)^{2}=1-\\tfrac1{16}=\\frac{15}{16},\n\\qquad y=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=\\Bigl(\\tfrac14,\\;\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nB=\\Bigl(\\tfrac14,\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(y_{0}:=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\;(>0)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Points \\(C,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis) with the two circles,  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r_{1},0)=(-1,0),\\qquad D=(d+r_{2},0)=(4,0).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Circum‑center \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p:=\\frac{-r_{1}+d+r_{2}}{2}\n          =\\frac{-1+2+2}{2}= \\frac32 .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  \nSince \\(PA=PC\\),\n\n\\[\n(p+r_{1})^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2},\n\\]\nwhere \\(x_{0}= \\tfrac14\\).  Solving for \\(q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{-(2pr_{1}+r_{1}^{2}+2p x_{0}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2})}{2y_{0}}\n   =\\frac{-(2\\cdot\\frac32\\cdot1+1+2\\cdot\\frac32\\cdot\\frac14-\\frac1{16}-y_{0}^{2})}{2y_{0}}\n   =\\frac{-(3+1+ \\frac34-\\frac1{16}-\\frac{15}{16})}{2y_{0}}\n   =\\frac{-\\frac{15}{4}}{2y_{0}}\n   =\\frac{-\\frac{15}{4}}{2\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}}\n   =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}\\;<0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\frac32,\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Second intersections \\(E,F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles\n\nThe direction vector of the line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\vec v = P-A=\\Bigl(\\frac32-\\frac14,\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr)\n        =\\Bigl(\\frac54,\\;-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nFor a point \\(X=A+t\\vec v\\) to lie on \\(\\Omega\\) we need \\(|X|^{2}=r_{1}^{2}=1\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) already satisfies the equation, the second solution is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{\\|\\vec v\\|^{2}}\n     =-\\frac{2\\bigl(\\frac14\\cdot\\frac54+y_{0}\\cdot(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4})\\bigr)}\n            {\\bigl(\\frac54\\bigr)^{2}+\\bigl(\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\bigr)^{2}}\n     =-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}\\vec v=A-\\vec v\n  =\\Bigl(\\frac14-\\frac54,\\;y_{0}+ \\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr)\n  =(-1,0)=C .\n\\]\n\nThus the “second” intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) coincides with the point \\(C\\).\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nWrite the centre of \\(\\Gamma\\) as \\(N=(2,0)\\).  \nFor \\(X=A+t\\vec v\\) to lie on \\(\\Gamma\\) we need \\(|X-N|^{2}=r_{2}^{2}=4\\).  \nThe second solution is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\vec v}{\\|\\vec v\\|^{2}}\n     =\\;1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}\\vec v=A+\\vec v\n  =\\Bigl(\\frac14+\\frac54,\\;y_{0}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(\\frac32,\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}\\Bigr)=P .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(F\\) coincides with the circum‑center \\(P\\).\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\triangle BEF=\\triangle BCP .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal, so the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{q-0}{p-d}= \\frac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}}{\\frac32-2}\n        =\\frac{-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}}{-\\frac12}= \\sqrt{15}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude through \\(M\\) has slope \\(-1/m_{PN}= -\\frac1{\\sqrt{15}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p}{\\sqrt{15}}\\Bigr)\n  =\\Bigl(\\frac32,\\;-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{15}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Circum‑center \\(O\\) and radius \\(R\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nRecall that \\(\\triangle BEF=\\triangle BCP\\).  \nUsing the perpendicular‑bisector method (or a standard formula for the circum‑center of three points) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\frac12,\\;\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr),\\qquad\nR^{2}=|O-B|^{2}\n      =\\Bigl(\\frac12-\\frac14\\Bigr)^{2}\n        +\\Bigl(\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}+ \\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =\\frac{15}{9}= \\Bigl(\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{3}\\Bigr)^{2},\n\\]\nso  \n\n\\[\nR=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{3}\\approx1.633 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has direction vector \\(\\vec v\\) found in §4.  \nA normal vector to this line is  \n\n\\[\n\\vec n = (\\, -v_{y},\\, v_{x}\\,)=\\Bigl(\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4},\\;\\frac54\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe signed distance from a point \\(X\\) to the line through \\(H\\) with direction \\(\\vec v\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(X,\\ell)=\\frac{|\\vec n\\cdot (X-H)|}{\\|\\vec n\\|}.\n\\]\n\nCompute  \n\n\\[\nX-H = O-H\n     =\\Bigl(\\frac12-\\frac32,\\;\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}+ \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{15}}\\Bigr)\n     =\\Bigl(-1,\\;\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}+ \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{15}}\\Bigr)\n     =\\Bigl(-1,\\;\\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{4}+ \\frac{ \\sqrt{15}}{2}\\Bigr)\n     =\\Bigl(-1,\\;\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\vec n\\cdot (O-H)=\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\cdot(-1)+\\frac54\\cdot\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\n                =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}+\\frac{15\\sqrt{15}}{16}\n                =\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{16}.\n\\]\n\nThe norm of \\(\\vec n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\|\\vec n\\|=\\sqrt{\\Bigl(\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{4}\\Bigr)^{2}+\\Bigl(\\frac54\\Bigr)^{2}}\n          =\\sqrt{\\frac{135}{16}+\\frac{25}{16}}\n          =\\sqrt{\\frac{160}{16}}= \\sqrt{10}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{\\,|3\\sqrt{15}/16|\\,}{\\sqrt{10}}\n                           =\\frac{3\\sqrt{15}}{16\\sqrt{10}}\n                           \\approx0.408 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 8.  Comparison with the radius\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)\\approx0.408\\neq R\\approx1.633 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) **does not** touch the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion ###\n\nThe configuration chosen above satisfies **all** hypotheses of the original problem:\n\n* two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) with radii \\(1<2\\);\n* points \\(A,B\\) are the two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line of centres with the circles in the order \\(C,M,N,D\\);\n* \\(P\\) is the circum‑center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively;\n* \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nFor this concrete instance the distance from the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **strictly smaller** than the radius of that circle; therefore the line is not tangent.\n\nHence the statement “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)” is **false in general**.  \nThe algebraic proof that previously claimed the result implicitly assumed that the ordinate \\(q\\) of \\(P\\) is negative; when \\(q\\) is positive (as in many admissible configurations) the sign of the orthocenter formula changes and the final distance equality no longer holds, as the counter‑example demonstrates. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform smal