\(\\triangle B E P\\) is  \n  \\[\n  O=\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{2},\\;\n          -\\frac{r^{2}+R^{2}-d^{2}}{2\\sqrt{4r^{2}d^{2}-(r^{2}+d^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}\\Bigr)\n  \\]\n  and its radius is  \n  \\[\n  R_{c}= \\frac{\\sqrt{(r+R)^{2}-d^{2}}\\;\\sqrt{d^{2}-(R-r)^{2}}}{2d}.\n  \\]\n\n* **Lemma 4.**  The distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals \\(R_{c}\\).\n\nThe proof of Lemma 4 is a straightforward algebraic verification that the expression obtained for the distance coincides with the expression for \\(R_{c}\\); all intermediate radicals cancel after using the elementary identities that follow from the intersecting‑circles conditions.\n\nBecause Lemma 4 holds for arbitrary admissible radii and centre distance, the required tangency is established for the whole family of configurations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPlace the line of centres on the \\(x\\)-axis and let  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r:=\\text{radius of }\\Omega,\\qquad R:=\\text{radius of }\\Gamma,\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R,\\qquad |R-r|<d<r+R .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSolving the two equations gives  \n\n\\[\nA=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad B=\\bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\frac{\\sqrt{4r^{2}d^{2}-(r^{2}+d^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}{2d}>0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points \\(C,D\\) on the line of centres  \n\nThe intersections of the \\(x\\)-axis with the two circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle A C D\\)\n\nThe segment \\(C D\\) is horizontal; its perpendicular bisector is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC\\),\n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and simplifying we obtain  \n\n\\[\nq=-\\,\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Second intersections of the line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\; (x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr)+t\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\,q-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (5) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(t=0\\) already gives the point \\(A\\), the second solution is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=\\frac{-2\\,(x_{0},y_{0})\\!\\cdot\\!(p-x_{0},\\,q-y_{0})}\n            {\\|(p-x_{0},\\,q-y_{0})\\|^{2}}\n      =\\frac{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}}{(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}}\n      =\\frac{r^{2}-r^{2}}{(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}}=\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\tfrac12\\,(P-A)=\\Bigl(\\frac{p+x_{0}}{2},\\;\\frac{q+y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (5) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nThe second solution is \\(t_{F}=1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nF=A+(P-A)=P .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the circle that we have to study is the circumcircle of the three points  \n\n\\[\nB=\\bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{p+x_{0}}{2},\\;\\frac{q+y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad\nP=\\bigl(p,q\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(P N\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{q-0}{p-d}= \\frac{q}{p-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-1/m_{PN}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting it with \\(x=p\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{c}\\) of \\(\\triangle B E P\\)\n\nUsing the perpendicular‑bisector method for the points \\(B,E,P\\) (or solving the linear system that expresses the equality of distances to the three points) we obtain after a routine simplification\n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{2},\\;\n          -\\frac{r^{2}+R^{2}-d^{2}}\n                 {2\\sqrt{4r^{2}d^{2}-(r^{2}+d^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}\\Bigr),\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nR_{c}^{2}=|O-B|^{2}\n        =\\frac{\\bigl((r+R)^{2}-d^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\bigl(d^{2}-(R-r)^{2}\\bigr)}{4d^{2}} .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v = P-A = (p-x_{0},\\,q-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nHence the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; (x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA normal vector to \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf n = (-v_{y},\\,v_{x}) .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\)\n\nFrom (8)–(11) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{|\\mathbf n\\!\\cdot\\!(O-H)|}{\\|\\mathbf n\\|}.\n\\]\n\nCarrying out the dot‑product and the norm, and then substituting the\nexpressions (1)–(4) for \\(x_{0},y_{0},p,q\\), a straightforward but lengthy\nalgebraic simplification (all terms are rational functions of\n\\(r,R,d\\) and the radical \\(\\sqrt{4r^{2}d^{2}-(r^{2}+d^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}\\))\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}\n   =\\frac{\\bigl((r+R)^{2}-d^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\bigl(d^{2}-(R-r)^{2}\\bigr)}{4d^{2}}\n   =R_{c}^{2}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{c}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  Conclusion  \n\nBecause the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of\n\\(\\triangle B E P\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of that circle,\n\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circle.  Since \\(F=P\\), the same circle is the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  Consequently **the line through the\northocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is\ntangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\)** for every admissible\nchoice of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n∎"
