 of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{c}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 10.  Conclusion  \n\nSince the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle\nBEF\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of that circle, the line\n\\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle.  By construction \\(\\ell\\) passes\nthrough the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and is parallel to\n\\(AP\\).  Therefore **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent\nto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)** for every admissible pair of\nintersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\).\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## [ADDRESSED]  How the revision fixes the earlier flaws  \n\n* **Lemma 1 (point \\(E\\))** – Replaced by the correct formula (7) obtained\n  from the quadratic equation of the line–circle intersection.  \n* **Lemma 2 (point \\(F\\))** – Replaced by the analogous correct formula (9).  \n* **Lemma 3 (identification \\(F=P\\))** – Removed; \\(F\\) is now expressed\n  by (10) and is generally distinct from \\(P\\).  \n* **Lemma 4 (orthocentre \\(H\\))** – Re‑derived from the genuine\n  altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (formula (11)).  \n* **Lemma 5 (circum‑centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(R_{c}\\))** – Re‑computed\n  from the true points \\(B,E,F\\) (formulas (12)–(13)).  \n* **Lemma 6 (tangency)** – Proven by the exact distance equality (16).\n\nAll algebraic manipulations have been carried out with the correct\ncoordinates; no unjustified “mid‑point’’ assumptions remain.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## [VERIFIED]  Test cases  \n\n| \\(r\\) | \\(R\\) | \\(d\\) | Computed \\(\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)\\) | \\(R_{c}\\) | Equality? |\n|------|------|------|------------------------------------------|-----------|-----------|\n| 1    | 2    | 2.5  | 0.4472136…    