,-0.9043),\n\\]\n\nwhich is **not** the point given in Lemma 3.\n\nThus Lemma 3 is also false.\n\n### ANSWER_IMPLICATIONS ###\nThe “solution’’ relies on three incorrect lemmas, so the algebraic proof collapses.  \nThe correct statement of the problem (the tangency) may still be true, but the provided proof does **not** establish it.\n\n### BOUNDARY_CASES ###\nBOUNDARY_1: \\(r=0.5,\\;R=1.5,\\;d=1.0\\) (still satisfying \\(|R-r|<d<r+R\\)).  \nRepeating the same calculations gives the same contradictions: the claimed formulas for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) place the points off their respective circles.\n\nBOUNDARY_2: \\(r=0.9,\\;R=1.0,\\;d=1.8\\) (near‑degenerate case where the circles intersect at a very shallow angle).  \nAgain the midpoint formulas for \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) fail; the computed points do not satisfy the circle equations.\n\n### ASSUMPTION_CHALLENGES ###\nCHALLENGE_1: The proof assumes that the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is the midpoint of \\(AP\\). This is only true when the line \\(AP\\) is a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\), which never occurs in the generic configuration.\n\nCHALLENGE_2: It assumes that the line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at the same point \\(P\\). This would require \\(P\\) to lie on \\(\\Gamma\\), contradicting the given inequality \\(r<R\\) and the construction of \\(P\\) as the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\nCHALLENGE_3: The derivation of the orthocentre \\(H\\) uses the (incorrect) coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\); consequently the expression for \\(H\\) is not the true orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n### CRITICAL_FLAWS ###\nFLAW_1: **Incorrect determination of point \\(E\\).** The algebraic step that sets the parameter \\(t_E=1/2\\) is based on the false identity \\(r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}-y_{0}^{2}=0\\) after cancelling the linear term; the correct quadratic yields a different value of \\(t\\).\n\nFLAW_2: **Incorrect determination of point \\(F\\).** The claim \\(F=P\\) ignores the fact that