nAdding (9) and (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' B' D' + \\angle C' A H' = 180^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the four points \\(B',C',D',H'\\) to be concyclic.  \n\n*Proof of the right angle.*  \nFrom (10) we have \\(\\angle C' A H' = 90^{\\circ} - \\tfrac12\\angle C' A D'\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle C' A D' = \\angle C' B' D'\\) by (9), we get\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' A H' = 90^{\\circ} - \\tfrac12\\angle C' B' D'.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause the quadrilateral \\(B' C' D' H'\\) is cyclic, the opposite angles satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' H' D' = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle C' B' D'.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdding (11) and (12) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' H' D' = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich proves (8). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Tangency of the circle \\((A H')\\) to the line \\(B'E'F'\\)\n\nRecall that the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is the image of the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) (see (4)).  \nSince \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is precisely the line joining the two intersection points of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) with the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nFrom (8) we know that \\(\\angle C' H' D' = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nBecause \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(AP\\) meets those two lines at \\(E',F'\\), the right angle at \\(H'\\) tells us that the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\) is tangent to the line \\(E'F'\\).  \nBut the points \\(E',F'\\) together with \\(B'\\) are collinear, so the same circle is tangent to the whole line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma.**  The circle \\((A H')\\) is tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Returning to the original figure\n\nUndo the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nThe circle \\((A H')\\) is the image of the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(