the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – The previous coordinate‑heavy proof relied on several false algebraic lemmas; consequently it does **not** establish the required statement.  \nThe problem itself is true, and I now give a completely different proof that uses an inversion at the common point \\(A\\) together with elementary angle chasing.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Invert the whole figure about a circle centred at \\(A\\).  \n   – The two given circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) become two straight lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   – The circum‑circle \\((A C D)\\) becomes the line \\(C'D'\\).  \n   – The points \\(E,F\\) become the intersections \\(E'= \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP\\) and \\(F'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP\\).  \n   – The circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) (which passes through \\(A\\)) becomes the straight line \\(B'E'F'\\).  \n\n2. Translate the condition “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((B E F)\\)’’ into the inverted picture:  \n   – The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); after inversion it becomes a circle \\(\\gamma\\) passing through \\(A\\) and the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\).  \n   – Tangency of the original line to \\((B E F)\\) is equivalent to \\(\\gamma\\) being tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'