the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the two original circles become\n\n\\[\nE' = \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP ,\\qquad \nF' = \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) also passes through \\(A\\); after inversion it becomes the straight line through the images of the other three points:\n\n\\[\n(B E F)' = B'E'F'.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSummarising, after the inversion we have the following configuration (see the figure).\n\n* two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) meeting at \\(B'\\);\n* points \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\), \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* the line \\(C'D'\\) (the image of \\((A C D)\\));\n* the line \\(AP\\) meeting \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at \\(E',F'\\);\n* the line \\(B'E'F'\\) (the image of \\((B E F)\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  The image of the orthocenter \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) its image.  \nBecause inversion preserves angles, the following orthogonalities are transferred unchanged:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&PH\\perp MN,\\\\\n&MH\\perp PN,\\\\\n&NH\\perp PM .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAfter inversion the lines \\(MN\\), \\(PN\\) and \\(PM\\) become the circles through \\(A\\) that pass through the pairs \\((M,N)\\), \\((P,N)\\) and \\((P,M)\\) respectively.  \nNevertheless, the **directions** of the three lines in (5) are preserved, and therefore the three right angles remain right angles when we look at the corresponding images.\n\nIn particular, the first orthogonality in (5) tells us that the image of the line \\(PH\\) is a circle through \\(A\\) that is orthogonal to the image of \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres of the original circles, its image is the circle through \\(A\\) that passes through the two points \\(M',N'\\) (the images of the centres).  \nConsequently the circle \\((A H')\\) is orthogonal to the circle \\((A M' N')\\).\n\nA more convenient description of \\(H'\\) is obtained by using the second orthogonality in (5).  \nBecause \\(MH\\perp PN\\), after inversion we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle\\bigl(M' A,\\, H' A\\bigr)=\\angle\\bigl(P' N',\\, A\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(P'\\) lies on the line \\(C'D'\\) by (1), and \\(N'\\) lies on the circle \\((A M' N')\\).  \nThus (6) tells us that the ray \\(A H'\\) is the external angle bisector of the angle formed by the lines \\(A M'\\) and \\(A C'D'\\).  \nA completely symmetric argument using the third orthogonality in (5) shows that the ray \\(A H'\\) is also the external bisector of the angle formed by \\(A N'\\) and \\(A C'D'\\).  \nHence the point \\(H'\\) is the unique point (different from \\(A\\)) that lies on the external bisectors of the two angles determined by the three lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ \\ell_{\\Gamma},\\ C'D'.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  A cyclic quadrilateral\n\nConsider the quadrilateral \\(B' C' D' H'\\).  \nWe claim that it is cyclic and that\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' H' D' = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of cyclicity.*  \nBecause \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\) lie on the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, the angle \\(\\angle C' B' D'\\) equals the angle between the two original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at the common point \\(A\\).  \nThe same angle is also the angle between the tangents to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at \\(A\\); after inversion these tangents become the lines \\(A C'\\) and \\(A D'\\).  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' B' D' = \\angle C' A D'.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow, by the definition of \\(H'\\) (see (7)), the ray \\(A H'\\) bisects the external angle between the lines \\(A C'\\) and \\(A D'\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' A H' = \\angle H' A D' = \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\,180^{\\circ}-\\angle C' A D'\\,\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAdding (9) and (10) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' B' D' + \\angle C' A H' = 180^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the four points \\(B',C',D',H'\\) to be concyclic.  \n\n*Proof of the right angle.*  \nFrom (10) we have \\(\\angle C' A H' = 90^{\\circ} - \\tfrac12\\angle C' A D'\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle C' A D' = \\angle C' B' D'\\) by (9), we get\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' A H' = 90^{\\circ} - \\tfrac12\\angle C' B' D'.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause the quadrilateral \\(B' C' D' H'\\) is cyclic, the opposite angles satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' H' D' = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle C' B' D'.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdding (11) and (12) yields\n\n\\[\n\\angle C' H' D' = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich proves (8). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Tangency of the circle \\((A H')\\) to the line \\(B'E'F'\\)\n\nRecall that the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is the image of the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) (see (4)).  \nSince \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is precisely the line joining the two intersection points of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) with the line \\(AP\\).  \n\nFrom (8) we know that \\(\\angle C' H' D' = 90^{\\circ}\\).  \nBecause \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(AP\\) meets those two lines at \\(E',F'\\), the right angle at \\(H'\\) tells us that the circle through \\(A\\) and \\(H'\\) is tangent to the line \\(E'F'\\).  \nBut the points \\(E',F'\\) together with \\(B'\\) are collinear, so the same circle is tangent to the whole line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma.**  The circle \\((A H')\\) is tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Returning to the original figure\n\nUndo the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nThe circle \\((A H')\\) is the image of the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\)