ince \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\)).  \nThe points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) are sent to\n\n\\[\nC'=\\mathcal I(C)\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑circle \\((A C D)\\) contains the centre of inversion, therefore it is mapped onto the line through the images of the other two points:\n\n\\[\n(A C D)'\\;=\\;C'D'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nAP' = AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the original circles become\n\n\\[\nE'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP ,\\qquad F'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) also passes through \\(A\\); after inversion it becomes the line through the images of the other three points:\n\n\\[\n(B E F)' = B'E'F'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSummarising, after inversion we have:\n\n* two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) meeting at \\(B'\\);\n* points \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\; D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* the line \\(C'D'\\) (image of \\((A C D)\\));\n* the line \\(AP\\) meeting \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at \\(E',F'\\);\n* the line \\(B'E'F'\\) (image of \\((B E F)\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The image of the orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) and put \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle P M N\\) we have the three altitude relations  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInversion preserves oriented angles; therefore each orthogonality in (6) translates into a statement that a certain pair of circles (or a circle and a line) are orthogonal.  Because the images of the sides \\(MN,PN,PM\\) are the circles \\((A M' N')\\), \\((A P' N')\\) and \\((A P' M')\\) respectively, the three relations (6) become\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\angle\\bigl( PH ,\\, MN \\bi