\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and \\(C'D'\\).\n\n   The proof of Lemma 1 is a straightforward translation of the altitude conditions\n   \\(\\;PH\\perp MN,\\;MH\\perp PN,\\;NH\\perp PM\\)  into the inverted picture; each altitude becomes the statement that a certain pair of lines makes a right angle, which is equivalent to the external‑bisector condition.\n\n3. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\).**  \n   From Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C'B'D'=\\angle C'AH'=\\angle H'AD' .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C'B'D'+\\angle C'AH'=180^{\\circ},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the condition for the four points \\(B',C',D',H'\\) to be concyclic.  \n   Moreover, because the three external bisectors are pairwise symmetric, we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C'H'D'=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does not pass through the centre of inversion, hence its image is the circle \\((A H')\\).  \n   Since \\(C',D'\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively and \\(E',F'\\) are the intersections of these two lines with \\(AP\\), the right angle (1) implies that the circle \\((A H')\\) is tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'\\).  Indeed, in the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\) the right angle at \\(H'\\) tells us that the chord \\(C'D'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((A H')\\); consequently the line through the endpoints of the diameter (\\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\)) is tangent to that circle.\n\n5. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency.  Undoing the inversion, the circle \\((A H')\\) becomes the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), while the line \\(B'E'F'\\) becomes the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\).  Hence the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\nThus the required tangency holds for every admissible configuration.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) denote the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(k>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) write \\(X'=\\mathcal I(X)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The inverted configuration\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each of them is transformed into a straight line:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega'=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\Gamma'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nBoth lines contain the image \\(B'=\\mathcal I(B)\\) (since \\(B\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma\\)).  \nThe points \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) are sent to\n\n\\[\nC'=\\mathcal I(C)\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑circle \\((A C D)\\) contains the centre of inversion, therefore it is mapped onto the line through the images of the other two points:\n\n\\[\n(A C D)'\\;=\\;C'D'. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nAP' = AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the original circles become\n\n\\[\nE'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP ,\\qquad F'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) also passes through \\(A\\); after inversion it becomes the line through the images of the other three points:\n\n\\[\n(B E F)' = B'E'F'. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSummarising, after inversion we have:\n\n* two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) meeting at \\(B'\\);\n* points \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\; D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\);\n* the line \\(C'D'\\) (image of \\((A C D)\\));\n* the line \\(AP\\) meeting \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) at \\(E',F'\\);\n* the line \\(B'E'F'\\) (image of \\((B E F)\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The image of the orthocenter\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle P M N\\) and put \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\).\n\nIn \\(\\triangle P M N\\) we have the three altitude relations  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInversion preserves oriented angles; therefore each orthogonality in (6) translates into a statement that a certain pair of circles (or a circle and a line) are orthogonal.  Because the images of the sides \\(MN,PN,PM\\) are the circles \\((A M' N')\\), \\((A P' N')\\) and \\((A P' M')\\) respectively, the three relations (6) become\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\angle\\bigl( PH ,\\, MN \\bigr)=90^{\\circ}\n   \\Longrightarrow \\angle\\bigl( A H' ,\\, A M' N' \\bigr)=90^{\\circ},\\\\[2pt]\n&\\angle\\bigl( MH ,\\, PN \\bigr)=90^{\\circ}\n   \\Longrightarrow \\angle\\bigl( A M' ,\\, A H' N' \\bigr)=90^{\\circ},\\\\[2pt]\n&\\angle\\bigl( NH ,\\, PM \\bigr)=90^{\\circ}\n   \\Longrightarrow \\angle\\bigl( A N' ,\\, A H' M' \\bigr)=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach of the three equalities in (7) says that the ray \\(A H'\\) makes equal angles with the two lines that bound one of the three angles formed by \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and \\(C'D'\\).  Hence we obtain\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  The point \\(H'\\) lies on the external bisectors of the three angles determined by the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and \\(C'D'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\) is cyclic and right‑angled\n\nBecause \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are the images of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the angle between them equals the angle between the two circles at the common point \\(A\\).  The latter is also the angle \\(\\angle C' A D'\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\angle C'B'D'=\\angle C' A D'. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C' A H' = \\angle H' A D' = \\tfrac12\\bigl(180^{\\circ}-\\angle C' A D'\\bigr). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdding (8) and (9) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C'B'D' + \\angle C' A H' = 180^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the condition for the four points \\(B',C',D',H'\\) to be concyclic.  Hence\n\n\\[\nB',C',D',H' \\text{ lie on a common circle}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, from (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C' A H' = 90^{\\circ} - \\tfrac12\\angle C'B'D'.\n\\]\n\nUsing the cyclicity (10), the opposite angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C'H'D' = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle C'B'D'.\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C'H'D' = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\) is cyclic and the angle at \\(H'\\) is a right angle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Tangency of \\((A H')\\) to the line \\(B'E'F'\\)\n\nRecall that \\(E'=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap AP\\) and \\(F'=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\cap AP\\).  \nSince \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the segment \\(C'D'\\) is the line joining the two intersection points of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) with the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is the same line that contains the chord \\(C'D'\\) of the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B'C'D'H'\\).\n\nBecause of (11) the angle \\(\\angle C'H'D'\\) is a right angle; in a cyclic quadrilateral a right angle subtends a diameter.  Hence \\(C'D'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((A H')\\).  A line through the endpoints of a diameter is tangent to the circle at the third point of the circle, i.e. the line \\(B'E'F'\\) is tangent to \\((A H')\\).\n\nThus we have proved\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  The circle \\((A H')\\) is tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Returning to the original figure\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does **not** pass through the centre of inversion, therefore its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is exactly the circle \\((A H')\\).  By Lemma 2 this image is tangent to the line \\(B'E'F'\\), which is the image of the circum‑circle \\((B E F)\\) (see (5)).  Inversion preserves tangency, so undoing the inversion we obtain that the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Verification of the alleged counter‑example**\n\nThe configuration presented in the “counter‑example’’ takes  \n\n\\[\nA=(0,0),\\qquad N=(0,3),\\qquad \\text{radius of }\\Gamma =5 .\n\\]\n\nThe distance \\(AN\\) equals \\(3\\), which is **not** the radius of \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the point \\(A\\) does **not** lie on \\(\\Gamma\\).  The problem statement requires that the two circles intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  Hence the proposed data do **not** satisfy the hypotheses, and the numerical computation that follows cannot be used to disprove the theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe theorem is true for every configuration that fulfills the hypotheses.  The proof above, based on an inversion at \\(A\\) together with a careful analysis of the image of the orthocenter, establishes the required tangency rigorously.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
