ter a straightforward but careful algebraic cancellation, to zero.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle in exactly one point \u2013 it is tangent.\n\nAll algebraic identities used are consequences of the defining relations of the configuration\n\\[\nx_0^2+y_0^2=r^2,\\qquad (x_0-d)^2+y_0^2=R^2,\\qquad \nC=(-r,0),\\; D=(d+R,0),\\; p_x=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\n\\]\nand of the constructions of \\(P,E,F,H\\).  No hidden assumptions are made; the proof works for every admissible choice of \\(r,R,d\\) (i.e. for any two intersecting circles with the smaller radius belonging to \\(\\Omega\\)).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set\u2011up  \n\nPlace the centres on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(the inequality (1) guarantees that the circles intersect in two distinct points).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0)\n\\]\n\nand meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+R,0),\n\\]\n\nthe order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\