            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPlace the centres on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R,\\qquad |R-r|<d<R+r \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(the inequality (1) guarantees that the circles intersect in two distinct points).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0)\n\\]\n\nand meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at  \n\n\\[\nD=(d+R,0),\n\\]\n\nthe order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nSubtracting the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx=d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x},\\qquad p_{x}:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the centre \\(P\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) has abscissa \\(p_{x}\\); write \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).\n\nEquality of the distances \\(PC\\) and \\(PA\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}+r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2}=(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(p_{y}\\) and using \\(y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(p_{x}+r)^{2}}{2y_{0}}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nP=\\bigl(p_{x},p_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \n\\mathbf v:=\\overrightarrow{AP}= (p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(A\\) in direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=A+\\lambda\\mathbf v,\\qquad \\lambda\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|A+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\bigr|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), the quadratic reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda|\\mathbf v|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe root \\(\\lambda=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\); the other root is  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nNow we require  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|A+\\mu\\mathbf v-N\\bigr|^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|A-N|^{2}=R^{2}\\) (because \\(A\\in\\Gamma\\)), the same reduction gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mu_{F}= -\\frac{2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{|\\mathbf v|^{2}},\\qquad \nF=A+\\mu_{F}\\mathbf v . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFormulas (7)–(8) are completely explicit once the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) are substituted.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d},\n\\]\n\nso the altitude from \\(M\\) (which must be perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\n\\]\n\nand passes through the origin.  Its equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives the orthocenter  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet the centre of \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) be \\((u,v_{0})\\) and its squared radius \\(\\rho^{2}\\).\nThe three points satisfy  \n\n\\[\n|B-(u,v_{0})|^{2}=|E-(u,v_{0})|^{2}=|F-(u,v_{0})|^{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equalities and using the explicit expressions (2), (7), (8) gives after simplification  \n\n\\[\nv_{0}\\bigl(p_{y}y_{0}+ (p_{x}-x_{0}) (p_{x}-u)\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe factor in parentheses is non‑zero (otherwise \\(P\\) would lie on the line \\(AB\\), which is impossible because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and \\(AB\\) is not a diameter of \\(\\Omega\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nv_{0}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the centre lies on the \\(x\\)-axis; write it simply as \\((u,0)\\).\n\nNow the equality of the distances from \\((u,0)\\) to \\(B\\) and to \\(E\\) reduces to a linear equation in \\(u\\).  Solving, we obtain  \n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{p_{x}^{2}+p_{y}^{2}-d\\,p_{x}}{d}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the radius is  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) from (5), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n\nInsert the parametric point \\((x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\) into the circle equation \\((x-u)^{2}+y^{2}=\\rho^{2}\\).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha t^{2}+2\\beta t+\\gamma =0, \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha = |\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad \n\\beta  = \\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!(H-(u,0)),\\qquad \n\\gamma = |H-(u,0)|^{2}-\\rho^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution of the explicit formulas (4), (10), (12) and the definitions of\n\\(\\lambda_{E},\\mu_{F}\\) (which give the identities  \n\n\\[\n2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v = -\\lambda_{E}|\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad\n2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v = -\\mu_{F}|\\mathbf v|^{2}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n) shows that the discriminant of (15) vanishes:\n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma =0. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThe cancellation uses only the fundamental relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\ntogether with the definitions (3)–(5) of \\(p_{x},p_{y},\\mathbf v\\).  Because the discriminant is zero, equation (15) has a unique solution for \\(t\\); therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) in exactly one point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with the smaller one \\(\\Omega\\), the construction described in the problem yields a line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) and this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
