{y}^{2}-d\\,p_{x}}{d}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the radius is  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}= (x_{0}-u)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nUsing the direction vector \\(\\mathbf v\\) from (5), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency of \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\)\n\nInsert the parametric point \\((x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v\\) into the circle equation \\((x-u)^{2}+y^{2}=\\rho^{2}\\).  After expanding we obtain a quadratic in \\(t\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha t^{2}+2\\beta t+\\gamma =0, \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha = |\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad \n\\beta  = \\mathbf v\\!\\cdot\\!(H-(u,0)),\\qquad \n\\gamma = |H-(u,0)|^{2}-\\rho^{2}.\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution of the explicit formulas (4), (10), (12) and the definitions of\n\\(\\lambda_{E},\\mu_{F}\\) (which give the identities  \n\n\\[\n2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v = -\\lambda_{E}|\\mathbf v|^{2},\\qquad\n2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v = -\\mu_{F}|\\mathbf v|^{2}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n) shows that the discriminant of (15) vanishes:\n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma =0. \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThe cancellation uses only the fundamental relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\ntogether with the definitions (3)–(5) of \\(p_{x},p_{y},\\mathbf v\\).  Because the discriminant is zero, equation (15) has a unique solution for \\(t\\); therefore the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) in exactly one point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with the smaller one \\(\\Omega\\), the construction described in the problem yields a line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) and this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
