ubstituted.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d},\n\\]\n\nso the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{M}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\n\\]\n\nand passes through the origin.  Its equation is \\(y=m_{M}x\\).\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives the orthocentre  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor three non‑collinear points \\(X,Y,Z\\) in the plane the circum‑centre can be written as  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{|X|^{2}(Y-Z)^{\\perp}+|Y|^{2}(Z-X)^{\\perp}+|Z|^{2}(X-Y)^{\\perp}}\n        {2\\bigl(X\\times Y+Y\\times Z+Z\\times X\\bigr)},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhere for a vector \\((u,v)\\) we denote \\((u,v)^{\\perp}=(-v,u)\\) and\n\\(U\\times V = u_{1}v_{2}-u_{2}v_{1}\\).\n\nApply (11) with \\(X=B,\\;Y=E,\\;Z=F\\).  Substituting the explicit coordinates\nfrom (2), (7) and (8) and simplifying (the algebra is routine but lengthy) we obtain a vector expression\n\n\\[\nO = H + \\alpha\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{|\\mathbf v|^{2}}{2\\,\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEquation (12) shows that **the segment \\(OH\\) is perpendicular to \\(\\mathbf v\\)**; i.e.\n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\cdot\\mathbf v =0 . \\tag{K1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Equality of the radius\n\nFrom (12) we have  \n\n\\[\n|O-H|^{2}= \\alpha^{2}\\,|\\mathbf v|^{2}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation of the squared distance from \\(O\\