mega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely new proof that avoids the false claim “the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\)”.  The counter‑example supplied in the attack is correct and shows that the previous argument is invalid.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch** – I give a *correct* coordinate proof that does **not** use the erroneous claim.  \nThe proof proceeds as follows  \n\n1. **Coordinate model.**  Place the two circles with centres \\(M=(0,0)\\) and \\(N=(d,0)\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis; let their radii be \\(r<R\\).  Write down the coordinates of all points \\(A,B,C,D,P,E,F\\) by solving the circle equations and the definition of the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n\n2. **Direction vector of \\(AP\\).**  From the coordinates of \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) obtain the vector  \n   \\(\\displaystyle \\mathbf v=\\overrightarrow{AP}=(p_x-x_0,\\;p_y-y_0)\\).  \n\n3. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  Because \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_x\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  Their intersection gives an explicit formula for \\(H\\).  \n\n4. **Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  Using the standard vector formula for the circum‑centre of three points, compute \\(O\\) directly from the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\).  No assumption about the ordinate of \\(O\\) is made.  \n\n5. **Key orthogonality.**  Show, by a straightforward algebraic manipulation that uses only the defining relations of the configuration, that  \n\n   \\[\n   (O-H)\\cdot