                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPut the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\qquad (0<r<R).\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect in the points  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad   \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\;(>0).                     \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).                               \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The point \\(P\\) – circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\np:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2},\\qquad P=(p,p_{y}) .                  \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe equality \\(PA=PC\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}=(p+r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\n\\]\n\nand using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\) (from (1)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}} .                     \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\dfrac{p\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr)} .   \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v =\\overrightarrow{AP}= (p-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}) .   \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor a point on the line \\(AP\\) we write  \n\n\\[\nA+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R).               \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin, the condition\n\\(|A+\\lambda\\mathbf v|^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe non‑zero solution is  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v .                                 \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nSince \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\(N=(d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n|(A+\\lambda\\mathbf v)-N|^{2}=R^{2}\n\\]\n\nleads to  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}},\\qquad\nF=A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v .                                 \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p_{y}}{p-d}\\); the\naltitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\),\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nTheir intersection gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\dfrac{p(p-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr)} .          \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nWrite \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  \nThe circum‑centre \\(O\\) is the unique point satisfying  \n\n\\[\n(O-B)\\!\\cdot\\!(E-B)=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\qquad\n(O-B)\\!\\cdot\\!(F-B)=\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.               \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8)–(9) and the identities  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |A-N|^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad\nA\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v =-\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{2}\\lambda_{E},\n\\qquad (A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v =-\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{2}\\lambda_{F},\n\\]\n\nthe right–hand sides of (11) become  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}= -\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{2}\\lambda_{E}\n      \\bigl(x_{0}+p\\bigr),\\qquad\n\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}= -\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{2}\\lambda_{F}\n      \\bigl(x_{0}+p-d\\bigr).                               \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (11) with the vectors\n\\(\\mathbf e:=E-B\\) and \\(\\mathbf f:=F-B\\) yields after a straightforward\ncomputation\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O=H+\\alpha\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp}},\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n               {2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} ,          \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\) and\n\\((U\\times V)=U_{x}V_{y}-U_{y}V_{x}\\) denotes the 2‑D cross product.\nFormula (13) shows that the segment \\(OH\\) is **perpendicular** to the\ndirection \\(\\mathbf v\\) of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;X=H+t\\mathbf v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]\n\nA vector orthogonal to \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\); therefore the\ndistance from a point \\(X\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(X,\\ell)=\\frac{|(X-H)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v^{\\perp}|}\n                                 {\\|\\mathbf v\\|}.            \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nUsing (13),\n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v^{\\perp}= \\alpha\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2},\n\\]\n\nand (14) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\frac{|\\alpha|\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|}\n                         =|\\alpha|\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\| .                \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The radius of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFrom (13) we have  \n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}=|O-H|^{2}+|H-B|^{2}\n        =\\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}+|H-B|^{2}.                \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation of \\(|H-B|^{2}\\) using (10) and (1) shows that  \n\n\\[\n|H-B|^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}= \\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.                     \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B,E,F\\) are three non‑collinear points, the common distance\n\\(|O-B|=|O-E|=|O-F|\\) is precisely the radius \\(R_{\\!c}\\) of the\ncircum‑circle \\((BEF)\\).  Hence, by (17),\n\n\\[\nR_{\\!c}=|\\alpha|\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\| .                           \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Tangency\n\nFrom (15) and (18) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=R_{\\!c}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle\n\\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the\ncircum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
