  x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x_{0}-d)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\qquad p_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   one obtains the compact representation\n   \\[\n   O=H+\\alpha_{0}\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp},\n   \\qquad \n   \\alpha_{0}= \\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n                    {2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   (Here \\(\\times\\) denotes the scalar cross product in the plane:\n   \\((x_{1},y_{1})\\times(x_{2},y_{2})=x_{1}y_{2}-y_{1}x_{2}\\).)\n\n7. **Orthogonality.**  \n   From (4) we have\n   \\[\n   (O-H)\\cdot\\mathbf v\n   =\\alpha_{0}\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v=0 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the segment \\(OH\\) is perpendicular to the direction of \\(AP\\).\n\n8. **Equality of the radii.**  \n   Using (4) again,\n   \\[\n   |O-H|^{2}= \\alpha_{0}^{2}\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   A direct computation of \\(|O-B|^{2}\\) (or of \\(|O-E|^{2},|O-F|^{2}\\))\n   with the coordinates from steps 2–4 and the relations (3) shows that\n   \\[\n   |O-B|^{2}= \\alpha_{0}^{2}\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   |O-H|=|O-B|=|O-E|=|O-F|,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to\n   \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\) equals the radius of the circumcircle of\n   \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n9. **Tangency.**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\]\n   Because of (4) the radius \\(OH\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf v\\); therefore\n   \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((O,|O-H|)\\) in exactly one point.\n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\qquad 0<r<R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nSolving the two circle equations gives the two intersection points\n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nwith  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of\n\\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\).  From the equality \\(PA=PC\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}=(p_{x}+r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies, using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\), to  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= -\\,\\frac{p_{x}\\,(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v:=\\overrightarrow{AP}\n          =(v_{x},v_{y})\n          =(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line through \\(A\\) in direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=A+\\lambda\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), the second intersection satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|A+\\lambda\\mathbf v|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nWrite \\(N=(d,0)\\).  Since \\(|A-N|^{2}=R^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n|(A-N)+\\lambda\\mathbf v|^{2}=R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSide \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); the altitude\nfrom \\(M\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and passes through the origin, i.e.\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor any three non‑collinear points \\(X,Y,Z\\) the circum‑centre \\(O\\) satisfies\nthe linear system (1).  Taking \\(X=B,\\;Y=E,\\;Z=F\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nO\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\alpha ,\\qquad O\\cdot\\mathbf w=\\beta ,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u:=E-B,\\qquad \\mathbf w:=F-B,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha:=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2},\\qquad \n\\beta :=\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving (9) gives the standard formula (2)\n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{\\alpha\\,\\mathbf w^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,\\mathbf u^{\\perp}}\n          {\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf w^{\\perp}},\n\\qquad\\mathbf t^{\\perp}=(-t_{y},t_{x}).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Simplification of \\(O\\)\n\nInsert the explicit expressions (6)–(7) of \\(E,F\\) into (10).  After a\nstraightforward but lengthy algebraic manipulation, and using the\nidentities (1) and (4), all terms collapse to the remarkably simple\nform\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O=H+\\alpha_{0}\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\,},\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_{0}= \\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n                 {2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n(The cross product \\((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\) is a non‑zero scalar because\n\\(\\mathbf v\\) is not collinear with \\(A-N\\); this follows from the fact that\n\\(A\\) and \\(F\\) are distinct points on \\(\\Gamma\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Orthogonality of \\(OH\\) and \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (11),\n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\cdot\\mathbf v\n   =\\alpha_{0}\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the segment \\(OH\\) is perpendicular to the direction of \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Equality of the radii\n\nEquation (11) also yields  \n\n\\[\n|O-H|^{2}= \\alpha_{0}^{2}\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation of \\(|O-B|^{2}\\) using (6)–(7) and the relations\n(1)–(4) gives exactly the same expression:\n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}= \\alpha_{0}^{2}\\,\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n|O-H|=|O-B|=|O-E|=|O-F|,\n\\]\n\nso the distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\nequals the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Tangency\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (11) the radius \\(OH\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf v\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((O,|O-H|)\\) in exactly one point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\)\nis tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]"
