s  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points we have  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1)–(3)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(2dr)^{2}-(r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}  \\\\\n&=\\frac{[(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}]\\,[r^{2}-(d-R)^{2}]}{4d^{2}}   \\\\\n&=\\frac{(R+r+d)(R+r-d)(d+R-r)(d-R+r)}{4d^{2}}  >0 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(y_{0}=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\) is a well‑defined positive number and the two\nintersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nIts intersections with the circles are obtained by putting \\(y=0\\) in the\nequations of the circles:\n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\), the perpendicular\nbisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(P\\) must satisfy \\(PA=PC\\).  Writing \\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\) and using\n\\(|PA|^{2}=|PC|^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}=(p_{x}+r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhence  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(p_{x}+r)^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y}),\\qquad \n\\mathbf v:=\\overrightarrow{AP}=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles  \n\nThe line through \\(A\\) in direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=A+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (7) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Because \\(A\\) already satisfies the\nequation, the quadratic reduces to  \n\n\\[\n2\\lambda\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe root \\(\\lambda=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\); the other root is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (7) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  Using \\((A-N)^{2}=R^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2\\lambda\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=0 ,\n\\]\n\nso that the non‑trivial solution is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} } .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore\nthe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\),\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr) } .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor three non‑collinear points \\(X,Y,Z\\) the circum‑centre can be written as  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{|X|^{2}(Y-Z)^{\\perp}+|Y|^{2}(Z-X)^{\\perp}+|Z|^{2}(X-Y)^{\\perp}}\n          {2\\bigl(X\\times Y+Y\\times Z+Z\\times X\\bigr)},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((u,v)^{\\perp}=(-v,u)\\) and \\(U\\times V=u_{1}v_{2}-u_{2}v_{1}\\).\n\nWe substitute \\(X=B,\\;Y=E,\\;Z=F\\) and use th