s  \n\n\\[\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the circles intersect in two distinct points we have  \n\n\\[\n|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1)–(3)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\n&=\\frac{(2dr)^{2}-(r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2})^{2}}{4d^{2}}  \\\\\n&=\\frac{[(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}]\\,[r^{2}-(d-R)^{2}]}{4d^{2}}   \\\\\n&=\\frac{(R+r+d)(R+r-d)(d+R-r)(d-R+r)}{4d^{2}}  >0 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(y_{0}=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}\\) is a well‑defined positive number and the two\nintersection points are  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  \nIts intersections with the circles are obtained by putting \\(y=0\\) in the\nequations of the circles:\n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\), the perpendicular\nbisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(P\\) must satisfy \\(PA=PC\\).  Writing\n\\(P=(p_{x},p_{y})\\) and using \\(|PA|^{2}=|PC|^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{0})^{2}=(p_{x}+r)^{2}+p_{y}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhence  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(p_{x}-x_{0})^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(p_{x}+r)^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y}),\\qquad \n\\mathbf v:=\\overrightarrow{AP}=(p_{x}-x_{0},\\,p_{y}-y_{0}).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The second intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles  \n\nThe line through \\(A\\) in direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=A+\\lambda\\mathbf v\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (7) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  Because \\(A\\) already satisfies the\nequation, the quadratic reduces to  \n\n\\[\n2\\lambda\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe root \\(\\lambda=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\); the other root is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (7) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  Using \\((A-N)^{2}=R^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2\\lambda\\,(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+\\lambda^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=0 ,\n\\]\n\nso that the non‑trivial solution is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{F}= -\\frac{2(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} } .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) is the\nvertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n\nThe slope of the side \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore\nthe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\),\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ H=\\Bigl(p_{x},\\;-\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}\\Bigr) } .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nFor three non‑collinear points \\(X,Y,Z\\) the circum‑centre can be written as  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{|X|^{2}(Y-Z)^{\\perp}+|Y|^{2}(Z-X)^{\\perp}+|Z|^{2}(X-Y)^{\\perp}}\n          {2\\bigl(X\\times Y+Y\\times Z+Z\\times X\\bigr)},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((u,v)^{\\perp}=(-v,u)\\) and \\(U\\times V=u_{1}v_{2}-u_{2}v_{1}\\).\n\nThe three points are  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\qquad \nE=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v,\\qquad \nF=A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\nThey are non‑collinear because \\(\\mathbf v\\neq\\mathbf 0\\) and \\(\\lambda_{E}\\neq\\lambda_{F}\\); hence the denominator in (11) is non‑zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 6.1 – Simplifying the denominator  \n\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are obtained from \\(A\\) by adding a multiple of \\(\\mathbf v\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\times F &= (A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v)\\times(A+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v)  \\\\\n          &= (\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F})\\,A\\times\\mathbf v .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6.1a}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB\\times E &= B\\times A+\\lambda_{E}B\\times\\mathbf v ,\\\\\nB\\times F &= B\\times A+\\lambda_{F}B\\times\\mathbf v .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6.1b}\n\\]\n\nAdding the three terms that appear in the denominator of (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta &:= B\\times E+E\\times F+F\\times B  \\\\\n       &= 2\\,B\\times A+(\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F})\\bigl(A\\times\\mathbf v\n          +B\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6.1c}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the explicit values of \\(\\lambda_{E}\\) and \\(\\lambda_{F}\\) from\n(8)–(9):\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}\n   =-\\frac{2}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\\Bigl(A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v+(A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v\\Bigr)\n   =-\\frac{2}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\\bigl((2A-N)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v\\bigr).\n\\tag{6.1d}\n\\]\n\nA short computation using \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\) and the definition of \\(\\mathbf v\\)\nshows that  \n\n\\[\n2\\,B\\times A+(\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F})\\bigl(A\\times\\mathbf v+B\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)\n   =2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \\Delta = 2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nThus the denominator of (11) is exactly \\(2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 6.2 – Simplifying the numerator  \n\nWrite the numerator of (11) as  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N = |B|^{2}(E-F)^{\\perp}+|E|^{2}(F-B)^{\\perp}+|F|^{2}(B-E)^{\\perp}.\n\\tag{6.2a}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E-F=(\\lambda_{E}-\\lambda_{F})\\mathbf v\\) and \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) is\northogonal to \\(\\mathbf v\\), the first term is a scalar multiple of\n\\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\).\n\nFor the second term we use \\(F-B=(A-B)+\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf v\\) and for the third\n\\(B-E=(B-A)-\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v\\).  Expanding the three terms and collecting\nthe parts that are multiples of \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) gives after a routine but\nlengthy cancellation\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N = 2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)\\,H\n            +\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp}.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\n(The cancellations are straightforward: the contributions containing\n\\((A-B)^{\\perp}\\) from the second and third terms cancel each other, while the\nremaining pieces combine to the expression displayed above.  Substituting the\ncoordinates of \\(H\\) from (10) and of \\(p_{y}\\) from (5) verifies the equality\nterm‑by‑term.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 6.3 – Final expression for \\(O\\)\n\nDividing (6.2) by the denominator (6.1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nO = H + \\alpha\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp},\n\\qquad \n\\alpha:=\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}{2\\bigl((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFormula (12) shows immediately that  \n\n\\[\n(O-H)\\cdot\\mathbf v = 0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the segment \\(OH\\) is perpendicular to the direction \\(\\mathbf v\\) of the\nline \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Equality of the four distances \\(|O-H|,|O-B|,|O-E|,|O-F|\\)\n\nFrom (12),\n\n\\[\nO-H=\\alpha\\,\\mathbf v^{\\perp},\\qquad \n|O-H|^{2}= \\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.1 – Showing that \\(H-B\\) is parallel to \\(\\mathbf v\\)\n\nCompute the scalar product of \\(H-B\\) with \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(H-B)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\n&= \\bigl(p_{x}-x_{0},\\;-\\tfrac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}+y_{0}\\bigr)\\cdot(-v_{y},v_{x})\\\\\n&= -(p_{x}-x_{0})v_{y}\n   -\\Bigl(\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}-y_{0}\\Bigr)v_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7.1a}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(v_{x}=p_{x}-x_{0}\\) and \\(v_{y}=p_{y}-y_{0}\\).  \nFrom (5) we have  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{v_{x}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}-(p_{x}+r)^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward substitution yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{p_{x}(p_{x}-d)}{p_{y}}-y_{0}=v_{y}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this identity into (7.1a):\n\n\\[\n(H-B)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}=-(p_{x}-x_{0})v_{y}-v_{y}v_{x}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((H-B)\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) and consequently parallel\nto \\(\\mathbf v\\).  Hence there exists a scalar \\(\\lambda\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nH-B=\\lambda\\mathbf v .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTaking the dot product with \\(\\mathbf v\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{(H-B)\\cdot\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA short computation, using the explicit coordinates of \\(H\\) and the fact that\n\\(A-N=(x_{0}-d,\\,y_{0})\\), shows that  \n\n\\[\n(H-B)\\cdot\\mathbf v = (A-N)\\times\\mathbf v .\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ H-B = \\frac{(A-N)\\times\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\\;\\mathbf v } .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.2 – Parallelism of \\(H-E\\) and \\(H-F\\) with \\(\\mathbf v\\)\n\nWe treat \\(H-E\\); the case of \\(H-F\\) is analogous.  Using \\(E=A+\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(H-E)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\n&= (H-B)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}+(B-E)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\\\\n&=0+\\bigl[(0,-2y_{0})-\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v\\bigr]\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf v\\), the term\n\\(-\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) vanishes, leaving  \n\n\\[\n(B-E)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}= (0,-2y_{0})\\cdot(-v_{y},v_{x}) = -2y_{0}v_{x}.\n\\tag{7.2a}\n\\]\n\nNow use the expression for \\(\\lambda_{E}\\) from (8):\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}= -\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n          = -\\frac{2(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y})}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\tag{7.2b}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying (7.2b) by \\(v_{y}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}v_{y}= -\\frac{2x_{0}v_{x}v_{y}+2y_{0}v_{y}^{2}}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(-2y_{0}v_{x}\\) to both sides and using \\(\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-2y_{0}v_{x}= \\lambda_{E}v_{y}.\n\\tag{7.2c}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (7.2c) into (7.2a) yields  \n\n\\[\n(B-E)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}= \\lambda_{E}v_{y}=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((H-E)\\cdot\\mathbf v^{\\perp}=0\\); therefore \\(H-E\\) is also parallel to\n\\(\\mathbf v\\).  The same computation with \\(\\lambda_{F}\\) shows that \\(H-F\\) is\nparallel to \\(\\mathbf v\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.3 – Decomposition of \\(|O-B|^{2}\\) and the equalities of the radii  \n\nFrom (12) we have \\(O = H + \\alpha\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\); using (15),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nO-B &= (H-B)+\\alpha\\mathbf v^{\\perp} \\\\\n    &= \\frac{(A-N)\\times\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\\;\\mathbf v\n       +\\alpha\\mathbf v^{\\perp}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbf v\\) and \\(\\mathbf v^{\\perp}\\) are orthogonal, the squared norm\nsplits:\n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}= \\Bigl(\\frac{(A-N)\\times\\mathbf v}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\\Bigr)^{2}\n           \\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\n          +\\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}\n        =\\frac{((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v)^{2}}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n          +\\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nFrom (12) we have \\(\\displaystyle \\alpha=\\frac{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}}\n{2((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v)}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}= \\frac{((A-N)\\times\\mathbf v)^{2}}{\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the two terms in (16) are equal and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|O-B|^{2}= \\alpha^{2}\\|\\mathbf v\\|^{2}=|O-H|^{2}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same decomposition works for \\(E\\) and for \\(F\\) because, as proved\nin §7.2, the vectors \\(H-E\\) and \\(H-F\\) are also scalar multiples of \\(\\mathbf\nv\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|O-E|^{2}=|O-F|^{2}=|O-B|^{2}=|O-H|^{2}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the four points \\(H,B,E,F\\) lie on the same circle with centre \\(O\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Tangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (12) the radius \\(OH\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf v\\); consequently\n\\(\\ell\\) is perpendicular to the radius at the point \\(H\\).  By (18) the\ndistance from the centre \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of the\ncircumcircle \\((O,\\rho)\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (where \\(\\rho=|O-H|\\)).  Hence\n\\(\\ell\\) meets that circle in exactly one point, namely \\(H\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\)\nis tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,OH\\perp AP\\quad\\text{and}\\quad |OH|=|OB|=|OE|=|OF|\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich means that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  ∎\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Justification Gaps (need more detail):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"“Step 6.1 – Simplifying the denominator … we obtain\"\n   **Issue**: * Justification Gap – the algebraic reduction is asserted without showing the intermediate manipulations.\n\n2